{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016cFZ2UhgRUowAjMcMprpUL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# mujs C -> Rust transliteration guide (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n2\t\n3\tWe are translating the C library in `c_src/` into a Rust cdylib in `src/`.\n4\tOne Rust module per C file: `c_src/src/jsfoo.c` -> `src/jsfoo.rs`.\n5\t\n6\tThe goal is a **mechanical, line-by-line transliteration** that produces\n7\t**byte-identical behaviour**. Do NOT redesign, do NOT \"improve\", do NOT fix\n8\tbugs, do NOT reorder checks, do NOT change messages or formats. Keep the same\n9\tfunction order and the same comments where useful.\n10\t\n11\t`c_src/` is READ-ONLY. Never modify it.\n12\t\n13\t## 1. File preamble\n14\t\n15\tEvery module starts with exactly:\n16\t\n17\t```rust\n18\t//! Translated from c_src/src/<file>.c\n19\tuse crate::jsi::*;\n20\tuse crate::prelude::*;\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\t`crate::jsi` has all types/constants/libc bindings. `crate::prelude` re-exports\n24\tevery non-static function of every other module, so you can call cross-file\n25\tfunctions by their plain C name (`js_pushnumber(J, x)`).\n26\t\n27\t## 2. Functions\n28\t\n29\t* A **non-static** C function `void js_foo(js_State *J, int n)` becomes:\n30\t\n31\t```rust\n32\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n33\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn js_foo(J: *mut js_State, n: c_int) {\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t  The `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` spelling with parentheses is required.\n37\t  IMPORTANT: every non-static C function must be exported this way; the linker\n38\t  symbol name must match the C name exactly (watch for `#define` renames in\n39\t  `utf.h` / `regexp.h`: e.g. `chartorune` is really `jsU_chartorune`, `regcomp`\n40\t  is really `js_regcomp` -- use the FINAL name as the Rust function name).\n41\t\n42\t* A **static** C function becomes a plain module-private function, keeping the\n43\t  same name: `unsafe fn jsB_helper(J: *mut js_State) { ... }`\n44\t  (no `#[no_mangle]`, no `pub`, no `extern \"C\"`) -- UNLESS it is used as a\n45\t  function pointer (see §7), in which case it must be\n46\t  `unsafe extern \"C\" fn` (still not pub, still no no_mangle).\n47\t\n48\t* Bodies of `unsafe fn` are implicitly unsafe blocks (edition 2021), so raw\n49\t  pointer dereferences need no extra `unsafe { }`.\n50\t\n51\t* C functions marked `JS_NORETURN` (and any function that unconditionally\n52\t  throws) return `!` in Rust: `-> !`. `js_throw`, `js_error_str`, ... are `!`.\n53\t\n54\t## 3. Types\n55\t\n56\t| C | Rust |\n57\t|---|---|\n58\t| `int` | `c_int` |\n59\t| `unsigned int` | `c_uint` |\n60\t| `char` | `c_char` |\n61\t| `unsigned char` | `c_uchar` |\n62\t| `short` / `unsigned short` | `c_short` / `c_ushort` |\n63\t| `double` | `f64` |\n64\t| `const char *` | `*const c_char` |\n65\t| `char *` | `*mut c_char` |\n66\t| `void *` | `*mut c_void` |\n67\t| `size_t` | `usize` |\n68\t| `js_State *` | `*mut js_State` |\n69\t| `enum js_Class` / `enum js_AstType` / `enum js_OpCode` | `c_int` |\n70\t| `js_Value.t.type` | `c_char` |\n71\t| `Rune` | `Rune` (= `c_int`) |\n72\t\n73\tAll struct/union/enum definitions already exist in `crate::jsi` with the same\n74\tnames and the same field names, EXCEPT the field `type` which is spelled\n75\t`r#type` in Rust (`(*obj).r#type`, `(*v).t.r#type`, `(*node).r#type`).\n76\t\n77\tEnum constants (`JS_TNUMBER`, `JS_CARRAY`, `OP_ADD`, `TK_IF`, `EXP_ADD`,\n78\t`AST_LIST`, `JS_HNONE`, `JS_READONLY`, ...) are all in `crate::jsi`.\n79\t`JS_T*` constants are `c_char`; all others are `c_int`.\n80\t\n81\t## 4. Statements and expressions\n82\t\n83\t* `a->b` -> `(*a).b`; `a->b->c` -> `(*(*a).b).c`; `*p` -> `*p`; `&x` -> `&mut x`\n84\t  as a raw pointer: prefer `std::ptr::addr_of_mut!(x)` or `&mut x as *mut _`.\n85\t* Pointer arithmetic: `p + 1` -> `p.add(1)` / `p.offset(1)`, `p - q` ->\n86\t  `p.offset_from(q)`, `p[i]` -> `*p.add(i as usize)` (use `.offset(i as isize)`\n87\t  if `i` may be negative). Never use Rust slice indexing on a raw pointer.\n88\t* Fixed C arrays (`char buf[32]`) -> `let mut buf: [c_char; 32] = [0; 32];`\n89\t  and pass `buf.as_mut_ptr()`. Indexing a fixed array with a Rust `usize` index\n90\t  is fine (`buf[i as usize]`), but if the C code can write past the declared\n91\t  length (flexible array members) you MUST use pointer arithmetic.\n92\t* Flexible array members have helpers in `jsi`: `js_String_p(s)`,\n93\t  `js_Property_name(p)`, `js_Iterator_name(p)`, `js_StringNode_string(p)`,\n94\t  `js_Buffer_s(b)`, `js_Value_shrstr(v)`, `js_Object_shrstr(o)`.\n95\t  `soffsetof(js_Buffer, s)` etc. are the `SOFFSETOF_*` constants in `jsi`.\n96\t* `while (*s)` -> `while *s != 0`; C truthiness must become explicit\n97\t  comparisons (`if p.is_null()`, `if x != 0`, `if !b`).\n98\t* `switch` -> `match` on the value with `_ =>` for `default`. Beware C\n99\t  fallthrough: replicate it explicitly. `match` arms in Rust need `c_int`\n100\t  patterns, so match on the integer directly:\n101\t  `match tok { TK_IF => {...}, _ => {...} }` works because the constants are\n102\t  `const` items - use them as patterns directly (they are allowed as patterns).\n103\t* `goto` must be restructured with labelled loops/blocks:\n104\t  `'label: loop { ... break 'label; }` for forward jumps, or duplicate a small\n105\t  block. A common mujs pattern is `goto readonly;` at the end of a function --\n106\t  translate as an inner labelled block:\n107\t  ```rust\n108\t  'readonly: {\n109\t      ... if cond { break 'readonly; } ...\n110\t      return;\n111\t  }\n112\t  /* readonly: */\n113\t  if (*J).strict != 0 { js_typeerror!(...); }\n114\t  ```\n115\t  Make sure the control flow is *exactly* equivalent.\n116\t* Integer overflow: the crate is built with `overflow-checks = false`, so `+`,\n117\t  `-`, `*` wrap like gcc. Keep plain operators. For shifts by >= bit width or\n118\t  by a variable that could be >= 32 use `wrapping_shl/wrapping_shr` only if the\n119\t  C code relies on it (mujs masks with `& 0x1F` already).\n120\t* Division/modulo of integers by zero would panic in Rust; mujs never does it\n121\t  on the integer path (JS uses doubles). Keep plain operators.\n122\t* Casts use `as`. Note `double -> int` in Rust saturates instead of being UB;\n123\t  that is the desired behaviour here.\n124\t* Comma expressions `return *idx = n, 1;` -> `{ *idx = n; return 1; }`.\n125\t* `a ? b : c` -> `if a { b } else { c }`.\n126\t* String literals: use C string literals, e.g. `c\"length\".as_ptr()`\n127\t  (type `*const c_char`). For a `char` literal use `b'x' as c_char` or\n128\t  `'x' as c_int` depending on context.\n129\t* `static` local variables inside a function -> module-level\n130\t  `static mut NAME: T = ...;` accessed directly (we are in unsafe code). Keep\n131\t  the same initial value. If it is a read-only table prefer `static NAME: [T; N]`.\n132\t* `sizeof(T)` -> `std::mem::size_of::<T>()` (usually `as c_int`).\n133\t* `memcpy`/`memset`/`strcmp`/`strlen`/... are declared in `jsi` -- call them\n134\t  directly, with `as *mut c_void` / `as *const c_void` casts as needed.\n135\t* `printf`, `snprintf`, `sprintf`, `fprintf`, `puts`, `putchar`, `fputs`,\n136\t  `fputc` are declared in `jsi` as C variadics; call them with\n137\t  `c\"fmt\".as_ptr()` and the same arguments (cast ints to `c_int`, keep `f64`).\n138\t  Never replace them with Rust formatting -- output must be byte-identical.\n139\t* Math: `floor`, `ceil`, `fabs`, `fmod`, `pow`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `log`, `sin`,\n140\t  `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2` are extern \"C\" from libm.\n141\t  `isnan(x)`, `isinf(x)`, `isfinite(x)`, `signbit(x)` are Rust fns returning\n142\t  `bool`. `INFINITY`, `NAN`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, `UINT_MAX`, `DBL_MAX`,\n143\t  `DBL_MIN`, `DBL_EPSILON` are constants in `jsi`.\n144\t* `isdigit`, `isalpha`, `isupper`, `islower`, `isspace`, `toupper`, `tolower`\n145\t  are in `jsi` (C-locale versions, taking/returning `c_int`, the `is*` ones\n146\t  return `bool`).\n147\t* `assert(x)` -> just drop it (the C release build defines NDEBUG). Do not\n148\t  add panics.\n149\t* `nelem(a)` -> the array length as `c_int`.\n150\t\n151\t## 5. Error / exception handling\n152\t\n153\t* `js_throw(J)` is `-> !`.\n154\t* The variadic error functions are macros in Rust (defined in `lib.rs`):\n155\t\n156\t```c\n157\tjs_typeerror(J, \"'%s' is read-only\", name);\n158\tjs_error(J, \"stack overflow\");\n159\tjs_rangeerror(J, \"invalid array length\");\n160\t```\n161\tbecomes\n162\t```rust\n163\tjs_typeerror!(J, c\"'%s' is read-only\".as_ptr(), name);\n164\tjs_error!(J, c\"stack overflow\".as_ptr());\n165\tjs_rangeerror!(J, c\"invalid array length\".as_ptr());\n166\t```\n167\t  Available macros: `js_error!`, `js_evalerror!`, `js_rangeerror!`,\n168\t  `js_referenceerror!`, `js_syntaxerror!`, `js_typeerror!`, `js_urierror!`,\n169\t  `jsC_error!(J, node, fmt, ...)`.\n170\t  They all diverge (`-> !`), so `js_typeerror!(J, ...);` as a statement is\n171\t  fine, and `return js_typeerror!(...)` also works.\n172\t  NOTE: pass integer args as `c_int` and pointers as pointers, exactly like C.\n173\t  A `%s` argument must be `*const c_char`; a `%d` argument must be `c_int`;\n174\t  `%g`/`%f` must be `f64`.\n175\t\n176\t* If the C file defines its own static variadic error helper (e.g.\n177\t  `jsY_error`, `jsP_error`, `jsP_warning` in jslex.c/jsparse.c), translate it\n178\t  into a non-variadic function taking the already formatted message plus a\n179\t  `macro_rules!` wrapper with the same name that formats into the same size\n180\t  buffer with `snprintf` and calls it. Example:\n181\t\n182\t```rust\n183\tunsafe fn jsY_error_str(J: *mut js_State, msgbuf: *const c_char) -> ! {\n184\t    let mut buf: [c_char; 512] = [0; 512];\n185\t    snprintf(buf.as_mut_ptr(), 512, c\"%s:%d: %s\".as_ptr(), (*J).filename, (*J).line, msgbuf);\n186\t    js_newsyntaxerror(J, buf.as_ptr());\n187\t    js_throw(J)\n188\t}\n189\tmacro_rules! jsY_error {\n190\t    ($J:expr, $($a:expr),*) => {{\n191\t        let mut msgbuf__: [c_char; 256] = [0; 256];\n192\t        snprintf(msgbuf__.as_mut_ptr(), 256, $($a),*);\n193\t        jsY_error_str($J, msgbuf__.as_ptr())\n194\t    }};\n195\t}\n196\t```\n197\t  (Declare such a macro *before* its first use in the file.)\n198\t\n199\t* `if (js_try(J)) { ...handler... }` becomes `if js_try!(J) { ...handler... }`.\n200\t  `js_trypc(J, pc)` becomes `js_trypc!(J, pc)`.\n201\t  These use real `setjmp`/`longjmp`.\n202\t* **Any C local declared `volatile`** (because of setjmp clobbering) must be\n203\t  accessed with the volatile helpers so the value survives the longjmp:\n204\t  ```rust\n205\t  let mut out: *mut c_char = null_mut();          // char * volatile out = NULL;\n206\t  vwrite(&mut out, js_malloc(J, n) as *mut c_char); // out = js_malloc(...)\n207\t  let p = vread(&out);                              // reading out\n208\t  ```\n209\t  Do this for every read and write of such a variable.\n210\t\n211\t## 6. Values and the stack\n212\t\n213\t`js_Value` is a `Copy` union: `(*v).t.r#type`, `(*v).u.number`,\n214\t`(*v).u.boolean`, `(*v).u.litstr`, `(*v).u.memstr`, `(*v).u.object`,\n215\tand `js_Value_shrstr(v)` for `v->u.shrstr`.\n216\t`js_Value` literal for `{ { {0}, JS_TUNDEFINED } }` -> `js_Value::undef()`.\n217\tCopying: `let v: js_Value = *js_tovalue(J, -1);`.\n218\tThe `JSV_ISSTRING(v)` / `JSV_TOSTRING(v)` macros are functions in `jsi`.\n219\t\n220\t## 7. Function pointers\n221\t\n222\t`js_CFunction`, `js_Finalize`, `js_HasProperty`, `js_Put`, `js_Delete`,\n223\t`js_Panic`, `js_Alloc`, `js_Report` are `Option<unsafe extern \"C\" fn ...>`.\n224\tSo `js_newcfunction(J, Ap_join, \"Array.prototype.join\", 1)` becomes\n225\t`js_newcfunction(J, Some(Ap_join), c\"Array.prototype.join\".as_ptr(), 1)`,\n226\tand a C `NULL` function pointer is `None`.\n227\tCalling through one: `if let Some(f) = (*obj).u.c.function { f(J) }` or\n228\t`((*obj).u.c.function.unwrap())(J)` when C would call unconditionally.\n229\tTesting: `if (*obj).u.user.has.is_some()`.\n230\tAny static C function used as such a callback must be declared\n231\t`unsafe extern \"C\" fn`.\n232\t\n233\t## 8. What NOT to do\n234\t\n235\t* Do not add `mod` declarations, do not touch `src/lib.rs`, `src/jsi.rs`,\n236\t  `Cargo.toml` or any other module's file.\n237\t* Do not define a function that belongs to a different C file. Other modules\n238\t  may still be empty stubs while you work; if `cargo build` says\n239\t  \"cannot find function `js_xyz`\" and `js_xyz` is a non-static function of\n240\t  another `c_src/src/*.c` file, that error is expected -- ignore it.\n241\t* Do not use `std::collections`, `String`, `Vec`, `format!`, iterators over\n242\t  raw pointers, or any allocation other than the C ones (`js_malloc`,\n243\t  `js_realloc`, `js_free`, `malloc`, `realloc`, `free`).\n244\t* Do not leave `todo!()`, `unimplemented!()`, stubs or omitted functions.\n245\t  **Every** function in the C file must be translated completely.\n246\t* Do not use `panic!`/`unwrap()` on data-dependent paths.\n247\t\n248\t## 9. Verifying your work\n249\t\n250\tRun `cargo build --release --message-format=short 2>&1 | grep '^src/<yourfile>.rs'`\n251\tand fix every error/warning that belongs to *your* file (ignore errors about\n252\tmissing functions from other C files, and ignore warnings from other files).\n253\t`cargo build` takes a lock, so if another agent is building, just retry.\n254\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NjgwbqE4zGKRSknZdUusmx","session_id":"6e63ba36-2d65-497f-a5b2-8fd6eb67db8b","uuid":"b89b5a72-ba60-4786-a9db-a73a16262f70","timestamp":"2026-08-22T21:24:00.516Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate jsdtoa.c to Rust"}
